WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:04.940
There are only three things you can count
on in this world: Death, taxes, and sequels.

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:08.760
And sure, most sequels aren't really necessary,
but they aren't always a bad thing.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.560
After a great movie, a sequel is like a second
date with our favorite characters.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:14.480
"Uh-uh.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:15.060
Second date.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:17.740
No tongue."

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:22.020
Unfortunately, we don't always get to enjoy
that sequel, even when the preceding movie

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.180
explicitly promised another installment.

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:27.460
Here are movies that teased sequels that never
happened.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:29.880
The Amazing Spider-Man 2

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.200
2014's The Amazing Spider-Man 2 made a lot
of promises.

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:37.280
It teased both a sequel and a Sinister Six
spinoff.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:40.240
Even worse, it ended right in the middle of
a backflip.

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:41.960
But there were plans for more.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.440
From scenes in the actual film to complex
end-credit Easter eggs, Sony was manning all

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:49.060
the guns to bring its extended Spidey universe
to life.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:53.780
And they probably would have if Marvel Studios
hadn't made Sony an offer they couldn't refuse,

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.020
spelling the end for Andrew Garfield and his
friends.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:04.610
The two studios agreed in 2015 to share joint
custody of the webslinger, freeing him up

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:07.939
to appear alongside his fellow heroes in Marvel-produced
films.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:12.420
The part was recast with Tom Holland, and
The Amazing Spider-Man 3 never materialized.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:14.380
Godzilla

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:19.380
Following the unprecedented success of the
MCU, practically every film studio was eager

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:23.920
to cash in with cinematic universes of their
own, but virtually none have had much luck,

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:26.600
with the possible exception of Legendary's
MonsterVerse.

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:32.590
2014's Godzilla and 2017's Kong: Skull Island
were both well-received, and 2019's Godzilla:

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:36.520
King of the Monsters looks to continue the
trend with an all-out Kaiju brawl.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:38.800
But this success was a long time coming.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:43.170
After 1998's Godzilla bombed, the big green
lizard's big-screen fortunes were essentially

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:45.200
crushed for the better part of two decades.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:55.420
Apparently convinced that this ill-conceived
version of the iconic beast was something

00:01:55.429 --> 00:01:59.799
audiences were going to want to see more of,
the filmmakers included a final teaser scene.

00:01:59.799 --> 00:02:04.979
At the film's climax, Godzilla lays eggs,
and our heroes must destroy all the hatchlings.

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.980
But the teaser reveals that they missed one.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.640
Unfortunately, the film's poor reception sank
any chance of a sequel, and it left the door

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:22.290
open for Legendary to acquire the rights in
2010, which so far seems to be working out

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:22.780
well.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:24.560
Kung Pow: Enter the Fist

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:29.100
2002's Kung Pow: Enter the Fist is one of
the weirdest films ever made.

00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:34.450
Comedian Steve Oedekerk began with a print
of the 1976 kung-fu flick Tiger and Crane

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:38.760
Fist, digitally inserted himself into the
lead role, added new footage to expand the

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.500
plot, and gave the world its first kung-fu
fight with a CGI cow.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.860
It's crass, juvenile, and awesome, and fans
have been waiting for the sequel teased in

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:56.820
its final scene.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:00.510
The bit plays like a trailer, teasing the
ongoing conflict with the aliens which were

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:04.160
revealed to be the first film's villains,
as well as the brawl that's sure to ensue

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:06.570
when the kung-fu cow returns for revenge.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:11.120
Oedekerk insisted as late as 2015 that the
sequel was still happening, but there hasn't

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:15.690
been a whiff of news since, making the return
of the Chosen One increasingly unlikely.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:17.640
"Your days are over mister."

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.240
Bubba Ho-Tep

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:24.980
Writer/director Don Coscarelli is best known
for the five films in his long-running Phantasm

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:29.400
series, but in 2002, he gave us one of the
single weirdest cult classics ever put to

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:30.400
film, Bubba Ho-Tep.

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:34.340
It's the story of an elderly Elvis Presley
who's forced to take action when his nursing

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:36.490
home is terrorized by an ancient mummy.

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:40.690
The film's ending seemed to set up the possibility
of future adventures, even teasing the title

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:44.320
Bubba Nosferatu: Curse of the She-Vampires
for the next installment.

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:47.300
But as Bubba fans know, there's no way it'll
ever happen.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:51.000
It's an unfortunate case of the film's star
shooting the sequel down.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:54.540
Bruce Campbell made it clear in an Entertainment
Weekly interview, saying,

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:55.540
"I killed it.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:56.540
I killed it for me.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:04:00.060
I feel that the first one was a nice little
gem and you don't have to make a sequel for

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.580
everything.…So, that one I'm just going
to let go."

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:06.580
Of course, Coscarelli could always make the
film with another star, but as far as fans

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:09.420
are concerned, without Campbell, there is
no Bubba.

00:04:09.860 --> 00:04:11.620
Hellboy 2: The Golden Army

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:17.019
The 2004 film Hellboy, an adaptation of the
cult Dark Horse comics character of the same

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:18.479
name, could have gone several ways.

00:04:18.479 --> 00:04:22.879
The half-human, half-demon superhero might
have translated very poorly to film, if not

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.200
for the extreme commitment of director Guillermo
del Toro and star Ron Perlman.

00:04:29.380 --> 00:04:30.900
"Didn't I kill you already?”

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:38.650
The film was a moderate hit and spawned the
2008 sequel Hellboy II: The Golden Army.

00:04:38.650 --> 00:04:42.599
The film teased further adventures with the
revelation that Liz was pregnant with twins,

00:04:42.599 --> 00:04:46.090
and del Toro spent the better part of the
next decade struggling to bring Hellboy 3

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:48.140
to life, but the dream eventually popped.

00:04:51.849 --> 00:04:55.660
The director confirmed via Twitter in 2017
that he was sorry to report that the sequel

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:57.420
was definitely not going to happen.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:01.200
After an outpouring of disappointment, del
Toro clarified that the door was open for

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:04.970
other filmmakers to move forward with the
property, and it looks like Universal is finally

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:06.599
going the reboot route.

00:05:06.599 --> 00:05:11.129
In 2018, imprint Lionsgate announced that
a darker, more horror-flavored Hellboy would

00:05:11.129 --> 00:05:15.440
be released in 2019, with Stranger Things'
David Harbour in the title role.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:17.780
Airplane II: The Sequel

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:23.000
There are few comedy classics more beloved
than 1980's Airplane!, the purest example

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:28.110
of the goofy comic sensibilities of the writing
team of David and Jerry Zucker and Jim Abrahams.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.040
"Can you fly this plane and land it?"

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:32.639
“Surely you can’t be serious."

00:05:33.060 --> 00:05:33.740
“I am serious.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:35.320
And don't call me Shirley."

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:41.300
It's an absolutely relentless wall of gags,
and 1982's appropriately titled Airplane II:

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:45.540
The Sequel exceeded expectations, even though
it just repeated a lot of the jokes.

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:47.100
"Elaine, screwdriver.”

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:55.960
There seemed to be no reason why Captain Ted
Striker and crew wouldn't return for a third

00:05:55.960 --> 00:06:00.439
go-round, and a post-credits title card promised
that Airplane III was on the way, but the

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:03.980
blame for this empty promise can pretty much
be pinned on Hays himself.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:07.860
Years later, Hays revealed that he'd simply
gotten sick of people pestering him about

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:10.349
the forthcoming picture, causing him to back
out.

00:06:10.349 --> 00:06:10.920
He explained:

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:14.240
"You have to understand the atmosphere, 31
years ago.

00:06:14.249 --> 00:06:18.599
Sequels were frowned upon....They were negotiating
for doing Airplane 3, and it had really gotten

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:19.100
to me.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:23.380
I was being ragged on by the press a lot when
I'd go to events....When finally I said, 'I'm

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:25.700
not going to do it,' that was it, they didn't
do it."

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:26.720
"Let's get out of here.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:29.220
Close it up.

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:30.720
Lights out.”

